  "current_findings": [
    "Regex dominates active CPU time within workers; transition construction drops from 56.34% at 2 workers to 9.52% at 4 and 1.29% at 8 in steady windows.",
    "Reader uses approximately 92-95% of one core at 4/8 workers, indicating a near-limit in the single read/JSON/normalization/broadcast stage.",
    "Steady worker active CPU is approximately 44.98 seconds at 4 and 69.06 at 8; repeated scans add work while full elapsed time improves only about 7%.",
    "Critical L1D miss stalls in workers are about 2.21/2.18/1.01% of instruction-processing cycles at 2/4/8; no separate proof of L2/RAM-bandwidth saturation.",
    "Equal rule counts do not mean equal cost; worker active intervals reveal substantial imbalance.",
    "Reducing each automaton greatly reduced transition-construction work for this corpus. At two workers, elapsed full-corpus time fell from 396.402 to 54.803 seconds versus request distribution. Adding more shards does not necessarily reduce aggregate CPU because all workers scan every request."
  ],
